# More Catting practice
>You can specify all sorts of paths as arguments to commands, and we'll practice some more with cat. In this level, I'll put the flag in some crazy directory, and I will not allow you to change directories with cd, so no cat flag for you. You must retrieve the flag by absolute path, wherever it is.

## Solve:
I first went to the home directory and then used cat to read flag with its absolute path
```
bash
hacker@commands~more-catting-practice:~$ cat /usr/include/drm/flag
pwn.college{AZP-La0n3FdT8pgx6U1oW5EEc__.QXwITO0wiM4ETOwIzW}
```
## Flag
```
put{AZP-La0n3FdT8pgx6U1oW5EEc__.QXwITO0wiM4ETOwIzW}
```

## Concepts Learnt
- Cat command of terminal and its uses with absolute path while not using cd.

